WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.400
JOE: When assessing and planning
for the care of a child or young

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.030
person with autism, they and their
parents should be able to express

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.039
their views, wishes and feelings.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.200
They should also be a part of
the decision-making process

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.920
which should always be made
clear and easy to understand.

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:20.580
The child or young person, and those
that know them best, should be able to

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.520
talk about what they have done, what
they are interested in, highlight their

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.320
strengths and capabilities and what
outcomes they are seeking in the future.

00:00:28.809 --> 00:00:32.129
BETH: When a child or young person
with Autism is transitioning into

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:36.629
Adult services and support, all teams
should work jointly to provide support,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.959
further assessment and services
that make that transition positive.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.729
Positivity is always important to help
the individual make better choices,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.129
try new things or learn new skills.

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:52.700
Diagnosis and management should be
reviewed throughout the transition

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.190
process, and there should be clarity
about who is the lead clinician to

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.350
ensure continuity of care and maintain
that ‘person centered’ support.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.700
JOE: If the individual is an adult,
families, partners and carers should

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.280
also have the opportunity to be involved
in decisions about support and care.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.600
Good communication between healthcare
professionals and people with autism and

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.750
their families, partners and carers, is
essential and time should be taken to

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.899
build a trusting, supportive, empathic
and non-judgemental relationship.

00:01:24.469 --> 00:01:28.019
BETH: This should enable support to
be structured to create an ‘outcome

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.809
focused’, co-ordinated plan, that
meets the needs of the individual.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:35.319
It should be supported by
evidence-based written information

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:37.209
tailored to the person's needs.

00:01:37.389 --> 00:01:41.629
It should also be accessible to people
with additional needs, such as physical,

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:45.839
sensory or learning disabilities, and
to people who do not speak or read

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:50.790
English, however if individuals do not
have the mental capacity, the code of

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.800
practice that accompanies the Mental
Capacity act should always be followed.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.439
JOE: Remember an important thing to
focus on with a person-centred approach

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.259
is not to see the ‘limitations’
someone may have, but to see them

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:06.419
as differences and as something the
individual can overcome with support.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.140
Showing empathy and compassion and being
supportive, can help to gain understanding

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.820
of the reactions and behaviours
someone with autism may experience.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.109
BETH: By being non judgemental and
not blaming the person helps us to

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.770
understand their needs and appreciate
any improvements to their social

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.730
skills and interactions with others.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:30.150
Treating someone as an individual,
can make them feel more comfortable

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.090
and supported, allowing them to
open up and take on new challenges.

